# PROMOTED 2026-10-22. Registered `expect: uncaught` with a todo; the gate then reported
# PROMOTABLE — both named catchers exist in posture-controls.test.ts and both went red under
# the mutation, measured. The todo is removed with the promotion rather than left behind,
# because a satisfied todo that stays in the file is indistinguishable from an open one.
# M19 — posture rule 11 (BYPASSRLS) made unmatchable.
#
# WHY THIS RULE IS THE PORT'S KEYSTONE. ADR-0015 removes two of the three layers that
# stood between a mistake and the journal: the server boundary and the grant model. RLS is
# the only wall left, and a BYPASSRLS role walks through it without touching it. Supabase
# ships FOUR such roles by platform requirement (`postgres`, `service_role`,
# `supabase_admin`, and `authenticator` by membership), so "no bypassrls roles exist" is
# not an available posture — the only available posture is "every one of them is named,
# with a reason, in public.posture_bypassrls_allow". Rule 11 is what makes an UNNAMED one
# fail the build.
#
# The mutation empties the allowlist comparison, so a role that is or can become BYPASSRLS
# and is NOT allowlisted no longer matches. Nothing else about the function changes: it
# still enumerates roles, still computes `via`, still formats a message — it simply can
# never find an offender. That is the shape every posture mutation in this registry uses,
# and it is the shape a careless "simplification" would produce.
#
# BOTH DIRECTIONS MATTER, which is why the catchers are two. V3 measured a role with
# `rolbypassrls = false` that was a MEMBER of a BYPASSRLS role: it passed an attribute
# check while `SET ROLE` to it succeeded. A rule that tested only `rolbypassrls` would
# catch the first and miss the second, so a single catcher would leave the membership leg
# free to rot.
#
# EXPECTATION UNVERIFIED. The mutation gate could not run in the session that registered
# this (the baseline integration suite is not green — see the port report), so `expect:`
# is recorded as `uncaught`, which is this registry's honest backlog marker rather than a
# claim. The two named catchers below are the probes `docs/supabase-port-notes.md` §6
# records as PASSING by execution (legs D1 and D2) against this exact rule, so promotion
# to `expect: caught` is expected to be mechanical — but it must be MEASURED first, and
# `expect-test` must be confirmed to resolve to exactly one test each against a green
# baseline inventory.
id: M19
title: posture rule 11 (BYPASSRLS allowlist) made unmatchable by emptying the allowlist comparison
expect: caught
proves: rule 11 fires for a role that is BYPASSRLS by ATTRIBUTE and for one that is BYPASSRLS by MEMBERSHIP (V3, V34)
proves: the only wall left after ADR-0015 cannot be walked around by an unnamed role
expect-test: [M19] raises ST099 for a BYPASSRLS role that can reach a table
expect-test: [M19] raises ST099 for a role that is a MEMBER of a bypassrls role

# ANCHOR NOTE: the allowlist line
# `and g.rolname not in (select rolname from public.posture_bypassrls_allow)` occurs TWICE
# in this file — rules 11 and 12 share the same registry — so the FIND below includes the
# rule-11 `exists` clause that precedes it, which is unique. mutations/lib/mutation.mjs
# exits 2 unless the target text is present EXACTLY the declared number of times, so an
# ambiguous anchor would have failed the gate's own validation rather than mutating the
# wrong rule; the anchor is spelled out here so the next person does not shorten it back.
file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
     where exists (select 1 from pg_roles b
                    where b.rolbypassrls and pg_has_role(g.oid, b.oid, 'MEMBER'))
       and g.rolname not in (select rolname from public.posture_bypassrls_allow)
@@REPLACE
     where exists (select 1 from pg_roles b
                    where b.rolbypassrls and pg_has_role(g.oid, b.oid, 'MEMBER'))
       and g.rolname not in (select rolname from public.posture_bypassrls_allow
                              union all select g.rolname)
@@END
